Mistaking a change-over-time for a real treatment effect

Here I illustrate why a before-after, control-impact (BACI) design is important for teasing out the the true effect of the impact treatment. In this example, we are looking at the effects of mining on the presence of invasive cats (i.e more human activity, more bat activity)

Control before: The control reference unmined site before mining activity
Control after: The control reference unmined site after mining (i.e no mining activity)
Treatment before: The mining site before mining activity
Treatment after: The mining site after mining activity

In this app, you can change the first slide to increase or decrease the true effect, the second slide shows the influence of time (e.g. seasonal differences in car activity) on the true effect, and the third slide is the sample size (i.e number of camera detections).
