shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)




How a single outlier or two pooled subgroups can fake a correlation.

A correlation looks real but is created by a single far-away point (outlier), or by two clusters that have been pooled together.
An example case of a single outlier could be user error (wrong number when recording), and you can test for this in week 5’s regression workshop (under model assumptions).
An example case of two subgroups causing a fake correlation is sexual dimorphism. lets say you are looking at the effect of body mass (x-axis) on the home range of animals (y axis). Without knowing the sex of each individual, you run a linear model (lm(home ~ mass, data = data)) and see there is a positive relationship between body mass and home range.
Problem: Your species is highly sexually dimorphic, where males are larger than females. When you account for sex in your model (lm(home ~ mass + sex, data = data)), you see that males (red circles) have larger home range than females (grey circles) regardless of body size.
