Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.

A sole reliance on p-values can misinterpret the results, because a non-significant p-value does not distinguish between the lack of an effect due to the effect being objectively absent (contradictory evidence to the hypothesis) or due to the insensitivity of the data to enable to the researchers to rigorously evaluate the prediction (e.g. due to lack of statistical power, inappropriate experimental design, etc.).
In simple words - non-significant effects could literally mean very different things - a true null result, an underpowered genuine effect, or an ambiguous effect.
A non-significant result is read as ‘no effect’. But a real effect studied with too few independent samples (see below app with n = 5 or 10) gives a wide confidence interval that includes zero - the study simply could not tell. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.
